@CsvSource({"1,one", "2,'two, x'", "3,"})
void csv(int a, String b) { System.out.println(a + "=" + b); }
It prints 1=one, 2=two, x, 3=null. Single quotes protect the comma. An empty unquoted value is null, and an empty quoted value '' would be an empty string.

when(m.find(anyInt())).thenReturn("a", "b");
System.out.println(m.find(1) + m.find(1) + m.find(1));
It prints abb. Consecutive values are consumed in order, and the last repeats.

| when(...).thenReturn | doReturn(...).when(mock) | thenAnswer
Type-checked return value | Yes | No (takes Object; a wrong type fails at runtime) | No (a wrong type fails at runtime)
Calls the real method while stubbing a spy | Yes | No | Yes (with when)
Void methods | Not possible | Use doThrow, doNothing, doAnswer | Via doAnswer
Result based on arguments | No (fixed values; several values run in order) | No | Yes
when(repo.findById(1L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(user));
doReturn("x").when(spyList).get(0);            // spy: real get(0) would throw
doThrow(new IOException()).when(sender).send(any());
when(repo.save(any())).thenAnswer(inv -> inv.getArgument(0));
Rule of thumb: default to when/thenReturn. Switch to the do... family for spies and void methods, and to thenAnswer when the result depends on the arguments.

Source | Arguments per test | Best for | Watch out
@ValueSource | One literal | A list of simple inputs | No null; add @NullAndEmptySource
@CsvSource | Several, inline | Input and expected-output tables | Empty value is null, '' is an empty string
@MethodSource | Any objects, via Stream<Arguments> | Complex objects, generated cases | The factory method must be static unless the class uses PER_CLASS lifecycle
@EnumSource | One enum constant | "Works for every status" | Use names and mode = EXCLUDE to filter
Rule of thumb: start with @CsvSource for small tables, move to @MethodSource as soon as the inputs are objects rather than strings and numbers.
